= Who decides <ch-power>

Part II ended with an economy that produces abundantly and a majority with a shrinking claim on the product. The market does not supply the missing claim. So the outcome depends on a decision, and this chapter asks who is in a position to make it.

At the end of @ch-history I listed three ways in which a population of people differs from a population of horses: people are customers, owners and citizens. Two of the three have now been examined and found wanting. Being a customer gives no protection, because production follows income, wherever the income is (@p-no-paradox). Being an owner protects those who own, and most people own little (@p-default). That leaves the third. People govern themselves, and governments can tax and transfer. What is that worth?

== Why majorities have had a share

@ch-economy said that a wage is a price, set by scarcity. That is half of how a group's share of output is decided. The other half is everything that law and government do to the result: taxes, transfers, the rules of the labor market, public services. The first half is economics. The second is politics, and it has its own regularity.

#hypothesis([Shares follow leverage])[
  Over the long run, how much of a society's output reaches a group through taxes, transfers and law reflects that group's leverage: what it is able to withhold from others or impose on them, including its votes where votes decide things. Entitlements that are not backed by some form of leverage tend to erode. _This is a generalization about history, with exceptions. My confidence that it describes the main tendency: about three in four. If it is wrong, and majorities keep their share by the vote alone, then the window of @p-window matters much less._
] <h-leverage>

The evidence for it is of four kinds.

*Wars that needed everyone.* The great expansions of voting rights, progressive taxation and social insurance in the rich countries cluster around the two world wars, when states needed the mass of the population as soldiers and as workers in war industries. A study of twenty countries over two centuries found that steeply progressive taxes arrived with mass mobilization for war, and did not arrive with democracy or with inequality alone @scheve-stasavage-2016. The top rate of American income tax was 7 percent in 1913, 77 percent in 1918 and 94 percent in 1944. Britain widened the vote in 1918 and built its welfare state after 1945.

*Workers who could stop production.* Bismarck created the first national social insurance in the 1880s to draw workers away from the socialists. In the United States, the laws that protected unions and created Social Security followed the strike wave of 1934. The golden age of rising wages after 1945 was also the high-water mark of union membership. In each case, concessions were made to people who could shut things down. Economists who have modeled the widening of the vote in nineteenth-century Europe conclude that elites extended it to head off unrest @acemoglu-robinson-2000.

*Plagues that made labor scarce.* After the Black Death killed a third or more of Europe's population, the wages of the survivors rose for generations despite laws passed to hold them down. The historian Walter Scheidel, surveying inequality across several thousand years, found that it has fallen substantially only after mass-mobilization wars, revolutions, state collapses and catastrophic plagues: events that either made ordinary people's labor scarce or put weapons in their hands @scheidel-2017.

*States that do not need their people's work.* Where a government draws its revenue from oil and not from taxing the work of its citizens, it has less need of their consent, and such states are on average less democratic and less accountable. The finding is debated at the edges; it is firmest for petroleum, and for the period since governments, and no longer foreign companies, have held the revenue @ross-2015 @notes-history. The slogan of the American Revolution runs in both directions: no taxation without representation, and little representation without taxation.

There are cases that seem to cut against the hypothesis, and they sharpen it.

Retired people are not needed as workers or soldiers, and in the United States they receive the two largest programs in the federal budget. Their leverage is the vote: they are numerous and they turn out. It helps that their benefits are universal, that recipients believe they earned them, and that the working majority that pays for them expects to collect in turn. So votes can be enough. But this is a minority supported by a working majority that has its own reasons to agree. It does not show what votes can do for a majority that no one needs.

The citizens of the oil states of the Persian Gulf are not needed for production either, and they are well provided for, while the migrant workers who do most of the actual work there are needed and get little. Need alone is not leverage. The migrants can be sent home; they cannot withhold anything. The citizens are few, they are the rulers' own base, and keeping them content is cheap. What they receive is at the rulers' discretion.

Put together, the cases say this. Leverage has three sources: being needed while being free to refuse; being able to vote in a system that honors votes; and being able to cause enough trouble that buying peace is cheaper. The working majorities of the industrial countries have had all three at once.

== What automation does to leverage

#proposition([Automation removes the leverage of being needed], from: [@h-leverage, @d-premise, @p-share-zero])[
  Under the premise, the first source of leverage disappears: work that is not needed cannot be withheld. The capacity to cause trouble weakens as armies and police forces automate and as the state's revenue stops coming from wages (@p-fiscal, below). What remains to the majority is the vote, and the willingness of institutions to honor it.
] <p-leverage-lost>

Two writers have named this prospect "the intelligence curse," by analogy with the resource curse of the oil states: if a country's income flows from machines and not from its people's work, those who control the state have less reason to invest in the people or to listen to them @drago-laine-2025.

The point is easy to overstate, and precision matters here. Formal rights are powerful. Courts, constitutions, elections and a free press are real constraints, and in the countries that have them they have survived severe tests. The claim is narrower. Until now, wherever democracy has existed, it has existed alongside an economy that needed the majority's work. We have no example of a democracy lasting for generations in which most citizens were economically unnecessary. Nobody knows whether the vote alone can hold a fair distribution in place indefinitely. It has never had to.

There is also an encouraging pattern in the same evidence. Where strong institutions were in place _before_ the windfall arrived, the windfall was shared. Norway found oil as an established democracy and put the proceeds into a public fund that is now worth more than \$350,000 per citizen. Alaska created a public fund from its oil revenue in 1976, and every resident has received a dividend from it each year since 1982 @alaska-pfd. Countries that found oil with weaker institutions, such as Nigeria and Venezuela, saw the proceeds captured. The lesson is about order. Institutions that are settled before the money flows tend to keep the money flowing to the public. Institutions that are built afterward have to be extracted from those who already hold it.

The loss of leverage is not even across the transition, and @p-cliff says why. While some necessary tasks still need people, the people who do them have _more_ leverage than before. The East Coast dockworkers, who can close a port, won a ban on fully automated equipment in 2025. Leverage first drains away from the many whose work machines can do, and concentrates in the few who hold a remaining bottleneck. It disappears for everyone only at the end.

#proposition([The window], from: [@h-leverage, @p-leverage-lost, @p-cliff])[
  The leverage of the majority is greatest before automation is far advanced, while most people's work is still needed, their taxes still fund the state and their numbers still fill the army. It declines as automation proceeds. A lasting claim by the majority on the output of the machine economy is therefore easier to establish early in the transition than late, and easier late than afterward.
] <p-window>

@p-cliff adds a complication. Early in the transition, average wages may be rising and statistics may look healthy, which is when the case for acting seems weakest. By the time the need is obvious, part of the leverage is gone.

== The state has its own problem

It would be a mistake to treat the government as an umpire standing outside the game. It is a player with a budget, and the premise puts that budget under great strain.

#proposition([The state must find a new tax base], from: [@p-share-zero, @p-wage-system])[
  The United States government draws 86 percent of its revenue from the income tax on individuals and the payroll taxes that fund Social Security and Medicare, and most of what those taxes fall on is wages. As the wage share of income falls, that revenue falls with it, at the same time as the demands on public support rise. The state must then tax capital, land, consumption or machine labor directly, or own income-producing assets itself, or borrow and create money on a scale that cannot last.
] <p-fiscal>

#plot("../figures/federal_receipts.svg", [
  *Where the federal government's money comes from.* Receipts in the fiscal year that ended in September 2026, by source. Total: \$5.4 trillion.
], source: [US Treasury, Monthly Treasury Statement. `models/fig_receipts.py`]) <fig-receipts>

The dollars that stop being paid as wages do not vanish; they turn up as profits and rents (@p-identity). But the tax system treats them very differently. By one careful estimate, income from work is taxed in the United States at more than 28 percent, and income from equipment and software at about 5 percent @acemoglu-manera-restrepo-2020. So every dollar that moves from wages to profits costs the Treasury nearly a quarter of a dollar under present law. And the payroll taxes that support Social Security and Medicare fall on wages alone.

This cuts in two directions, and both matter for the forecasts.

It means the transition will squeeze the public finances whether or not politicians wish to address inequality. Falling payroll-tax receipts will threaten Social Security and Medicare, the two most popular programs in American government, on which the most reliable voters depend. The state will be pushed to go where the money is. So there will very probably be taxes on the machine economy. The open question is what is done with the proceeds.

It also means the state changes its nature. A government that lives on taxes paid by a hundred and sixty million workers has to care, at least a little, whether those workers prosper. A government that lives on taxes paid by a few hundred firms that own the machines is a different kind of thing. It has every reason to care whether those firms prosper, and its concern for everyone else rests on the vote alone. This is the mechanism behind the resource curse, and it does not require anyone to be wicked.

There is one more fact, and it is mostly favorable. Much of the machine economy is unusually easy to tax. A great deal of twenty-first-century wealth has been hard for governments to reach because it is intangible and mobile: a patent can be moved to Ireland with a signature. The physical capital of the machine economy is the opposite. It consists of data centers the size of towns, power plants, chip factories and fleets of robots. It sits on land, draws power from a grid, needs permits, and can be seen from orbit. It also depends on the state at every turn, for land, power, security and law. The qualification is that mental machine labor can be delivered over a wire from another country. About seven tenths of the world's AI computing capacity is in the United States today, which gives the American government unusual reach. A smaller country would have to tax machine work where it is used, and not where it is produced.

== Sharing is affordable

Suppose the political will existed. Could a post-labor economy afford to provide for everyone? The arithmetic is short.

There are about 270 million adults in the United States. To pay every one of them \$1,000 a month would cost about \$3.2 trillion a year. That is roughly a tenth of the present output of the economy, and less than the federal government collects from the income tax and payroll taxes. Part of it would replace existing programs. If the economy doubled in size, as even cautious forecasts of the premise allow, the same payment would take a twentieth of output. And @p-prices says that what the payment buys would grow over time, because the things machines make get cheaper.

A thousand dollars a month is not comfort at today's prices; it is below the poverty line. It becomes security only as machine-made goods get cheap, and only for someone who has a place to live. There is also a cost that the simple arithmetic leaves out: in an economy that grows by reinvesting its profits, a tax on profits slows growth somewhat. Neither point changes the order of magnitude.

#proposition([Providing for everyone takes a small and shrinking share of output], from: [@p-output-rises, @p-prices])[
  In a post-labor economy, an income sufficient for material security can be given to every person at a cost that is a modest fraction of total output and that falls as the economy grows. The obstacle to providing for everyone is not a shortage of resources.
] <p-affordable>

This is the most hopeful result in the guide, and it should be given its full weight. In every earlier society, lifting everyone out of want would have required the better-off to give up a great deal, because there was not enough to go round. In the post-labor economy the owners of the machines could provide for everyone else out of a small part of the _growth_ in their income, and still become richer every year than anyone has ever been. Self-interest alone gives them reasons to do it. Historically, though, elites have often failed to make cheap concessions in time, and the cheapness of sharing does not guarantee that it happens.

== Who owns the machine economy?

A final fact shapes the politics. "The owners" are not five men in California.

Ownership of the leading AI companies and of the firms that supply them is held through the stock market and private funds, and so belongs mostly to the same households that own everything else: about half to the wealthiest 1 percent and most of the rest to the next 9 percent (@tab-wealth). That upper tenth is some thirteen million households. They are the default beneficiaries of the premise.

Below them is a much larger group, roughly the households between the middle and the top tenth, who are the first casualties. This is where most lawyers, engineers, managers, analysts, accountants and administrators are found. They hold 29 percent of the nation's wealth, most of it in houses and retirement accounts, and their current income is almost entirely salary. @ch-two-transitions will show that mental work is automated before physical work. So the first large group to lose its earning power will be the most educated, articulate and organized part of the electorate, with enough assets to feel what they are losing and too few to live on.

That is a very different political event from the decline of factory towns after 2000, which fell on people with little voice and was ignored for fifteen years. It is the main reason I expect the political response to automation to come faster than the response to earlier shocks. It is less clear what that response will ask for. A class that owns houses and holds licenses may put its weight behind protecting both, which is a different thing from spreading ownership of the machines.

#checkpoint[
  - Majorities have had a share of output because they were needed and free to refuse, because they could vote, and because they could cause trouble (@h-leverage). Automation removes the first, weakens the last, and leaves the vote to do work it has never had to do alone (@p-leverage-lost).
  - Leverage is greatest early. Lasting claims are easier to establish during the transition than after it (@p-window), and the transition is when average statistics may still look fine.
  - The state will be pushed to tax the machine economy (@p-fiscal), much of which is physically easy to tax. What it does with the proceeds is not forced.
  - Providing for everyone would take a small share of output (@p-affordable). The constraint is political.
  - The first to lose their earnings will be the salaried middle and upper-middle class, which has unusual political weight.
  - The next chapter sets out the choices.
]
